#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "98bc19b4-ab9d-51e3-9190-965bdc787845" :question/math-academy-id "e-7366" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b6739458c31d4ce752549f42083a66dc7a0a0914ac210837d0a041019d914c3.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sin\\left( 2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind an integral that represents the total area bounded by the polar curve." :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2 \\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\nA_{\\textrm{total}}=4\\cdot A = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/8}^{3\\pi/8} \\sin^2\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157100 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "5caa3fbc-f232-5e40-b6d2-c34fa552d6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7367" :question/problem "Find the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sin(7\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9aa4ff23010048536fbcbadc9cff5dc2fc094895c00814dd9efccdc9750682a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157101 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "a994968f-db5b-57f7-b836-f3ec295bfd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-7368" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e89c77448446dccb3ececd6f05f1a2e410f18f5077e50c88144cd2101cec9d54.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\cos\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nBy changing the limits of integration, find an integral expression representing the total area bounded by the polar curve." :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/4} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ and the length of this interval is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n![](images/b9/b98a92acf42831b84597662f03865f0c5bdb831af0573206c56f33ee6f3528ae.svg)\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ to $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA_\\textrm{total} = 2 \\int_{-\\pi/12}^{11\\pi/12} \\cos^2\\left(3\\theta-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5450b792-cfd2-5428-85cb-6e721aad8477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544273} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac577e2-de00-593f-842f-889f13634e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544386} {:answer/id #uuid "b86b5b0e-81d1-54fb-8de3-463acc2dbc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec10ba12-c948-5373-9400-a4298e8eaf68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544598} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffc359f4-c504-5c02-9fa5-ef8786493295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544647} :answer-field/id #uuid "835941de-1b21-50b1-bc5e-e054f925540b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "623b63a4-baae-557b-86dd-761ba0b029fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-124682" :question/problem "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25444643-934c-52ab-b890-db2ee6187d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544169} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b7463f-7f4d-5f03-a9e1-2942e0699bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544505} {:answer/id #uuid "e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544584} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c3f927-4c06-5422-a227-69b04f7920cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544615} {:answer/id #uuid "f6289bae-3a41-5c9e-8f1d-1c68a61a70d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7711dd9-3cc9-51a7-ba89-7107451ca153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544584} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c411aaa-ddaa-5464-8abb-dd307e74030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" :question/math-academy-id "q-124684" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544143} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd2fcc1-904f-5624-a891-21600d6a3559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544159} {:answer/id #uuid "319af86b-63e6-5b3a-8f84-af301c7e96fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544198} {:answer/id #uuid "5de93751-8e37-5c0e-ade7-b00a0286b1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544291} {:answer/id #uuid "b62a2b7d-1d9a-5e11-8aa5-b6a9b07a1289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b33f10d-c2bd-51b2-9314-14748e5b22b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544143} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f2fe27-3752-50bd-8e74-45d47d4357a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9182ab7c-62c2-510f-9183-c91606315f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-125329" :question/problem "![](images/94/94a5da0925a82b91d345fa7811e41c219c19ca48d76b30927e920efed3b2edb3.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{11\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544216} {:answer/id #uuid "45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{9\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544240} {:answer/id #uuid "4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544251} {:answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" :question/math-academy-id "q-125695" :question/problem "![](images/17/172737d1a5b28b9a60958dc7e6b1748db1912021b7df4ad26045648063037fd8.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{8}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544088} {:answer/id #uuid "1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544135} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544249} {:answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} {:answer/id #uuid "dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{3\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125863" :question/problem "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{th}}\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "788347b9-f328-50bb-9f8d-44dd99b00eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544354} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ca41cb-2481-5f25-ab16-b237d166c5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f4b1de5-2584-553e-ad73-4df20f726215" :question/math-academy-id "q-351293" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8afd0a30a2796cc1590e97c8d71e1d47128ac1993d2875072877149631642e5.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a984d6-e24d-5ad2-9d64-1bf9b0d07a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544382} :answer-field/id #uuid "972f7a9a-9438-5af7-a972-1e1761110b8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "950e52f2-cc86-53ef-af25-6a46ca25d518" :question/math-academy-id "q-351393" :question/problem "![](images/21/219480e656f2f5913d7a83d93871c09b334600365d46a041ae63607d3f2f8326.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043e78d4-5f01-563f-8dd0-f38020b32e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544095} :answer-field/id #uuid "be158308-89e7-524f-868f-9564cb669864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b75e23ab-5c6f-5e0d-91f4-1cb8debf07f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-351400" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcec4003e842d279dbed89307ef84a230b16443fe515a8cc621aea72bf8829d3.png)\n\nFind the total area ${A}_{\\text{total}}$ bounded by the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{total}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45626ab5-23ff-5b54-ab30-6536c8857200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544239} {:answer/id #uuid "5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544266} {:answer/id #uuid "53f41015-0e83-5163-bbaa-f1bb9ea38cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544272} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0b241e-150f-5225-9356-c59f965a081e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544327} {:answer/id #uuid "e116c89d-0721-51ca-8376-6b9644097c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5227f4d9-586f-5a87-96f1-2d29829a5051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544266} :answer-field/id #uuid "277f62b5-5b05-5cc1-9927-317efd86b1d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c5bc2e-d431-58fd-be86-bcbb846410a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83365" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09043c6b-599d-5c5d-ac4b-20dd6275a8f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a603b1-e7b9-5111-9287-7aa72828eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544089} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544147} {:answer/id #uuid "527f5534-b9ff-58e8-814b-8cf2eb2ab2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544267} {:answer/id #uuid "5f450753-49cb-5b4a-a620-d8dc65d78d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544293} {:answer/id #uuid "a418680f-7ad8-563a-ace3-88e406955935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca96453-2812-54b1-ac9f-2bc70296b399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544147} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba35f265-6454-54f3-bbaa-3ac2948921cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "527ee65c-de13-51b9-8ea3-2fa79ecc1df6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83369" :question/problem "![](images/47/47eb2f1073f62129ba2fbf7ae154b9733ff2d5bf09af30053f7b708d7a8d21b0.png)\n\nFind the total area bounded by the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544329} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544465} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544590} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544597} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-83484" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} {:answer/id #uuid "3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544230} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544444} {:answer/id #uuid "f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544616} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83485" :question/problem "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efbdd8f-088c-54e2-94f2-33585aa4d9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544155} {:answer/id #uuid "3eedec8a-d18b-5b88-83c4-00e34bb3b972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544229} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544464} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a6b41e-820d-56bf-ae6b-fd6bae64c165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544613} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fb72b-e66d-5acb-a71a-2e252f1c84fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b26a6ab9-c41c-52e2-907b-4ffdb43b0d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544464} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c60208-16e5-5779-9065-2feb76aee1f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e2ee0d0-657b-565c-90c3-ae6c29ac6b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83516" :question/problem "![](images/14/140947c29667712d4a1071305396fff56a24f0a5c11f26e31368b8cc011f2863.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 4=8$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=8\\cdot A=12{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c0740-2003-5c99-84f2-294c88d57ba7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e