c06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff8ea5a-73f9-53b1-aa2b-d89a1b062fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544160} {:answer/id #uuid "567b1d6d-20c9-5e7e-ad5c-e9bbd06e3774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544278} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544481} {:answer/id #uuid "df677947-f1f2-54d0-b9ed-85515727e9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544570} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc3e69-6223-5ab4-87cd-e8f77504d1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{4\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a93243-035f-54e5-a2ed-ba2740b32501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544481} :answer-field/id #uuid "676f7b35-5434-55eb-ad55-37dab460907b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7ae6625-2462-5c24-ab37-c40b68057a97" :question/math-academy-id "q-83527" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}]}
